Whisper: hold, check and read-back for content clipboard writes

What: nsBaseClipboard::SetDataImpl, the one place every content clipboard write passes in
the parent process, calls mozilla::ClipboardGuard::Guard() for the first
(content-initiated) pass. The guard (new files in toolkit/components/clipguard) classifies
the text with the clipguard Rust crate and either lets the write through, removes a
trailing line break, or replaces it with a harmless placeholder while holding the real
text for "Copy anyway". The clipboard read paths (GetDataIfSmallerThanNative,
GetDataSnapshot, GetDataSnapshotSync) also ask the guard first: while a block is in effect,
the page that was blocked -- and only that page (same top browsing context and origin) --
reads back its own written text instead of the placeholder, so it cannot tell it was
blocked; every other reader gets the placeholder. nsBaseClipboard grants the guard and its
event object friendship so they can commit a held write through the existing
SetDataImpl(aCheckContentAnalysis = false) path, the same primitive content analysis uses.
A real content write forgets the read-back override.
Why: the hold/placeholder/false-success machinery reuses PendingCopy, present since Firefox
158. The page never learns the decision: its write always succeeds, and a read-back probe
gets its own text. nsIClipguardEvent carries the decision to the interface. The file was
last changed in September 2026 (bug 2057113, bug 2067877); keep this patch small so an
engine update rebases cleanly.
